-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## Learning Objectives
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC By the end of this lesson, you will be able to:
-- MAGIC - Create and assign metastores
-- MAGIC - Delegate the metastore admin role
-- MAGIC - Configure compute resources for UC compatibility

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## A. Managing Metastores
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC A **metastore** is a persistent metadata repository that records information about data assets and the policies that govern access to them.  It typically stores object definitions and properties for items like schemas (often referred to as databases), tables, views, and authorized storage locations, along with ownership and permissions used by the platform to authorize reads and writes.  
-- MAGIC Operationally, it provides a common namespace for discovery and consistent references to data, allowing administrators to manage policies centrally while engines use the metadata to resolve and validate data operations.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### A1. Comparison of Hive Metastore to Unity Catalog
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Unity Catalog presents a significantly evolved metastore architecture and security model, and it's important to have a clear understanding of the architectural difference between the legacy Hive Metastore (HMS) and Unity Catalog in order to understand the benefits and to implement it effectively into your account.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC #### A1.1 Life Before UC
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC The legacy Hive metastore in Databricks was organized as a per-workspace, cluster-scoped metadata repository. Each workspace had its own Hive metastore instance, responsible for storing metadata about databases, tables, views, and permissions. Access controls and table permissions applied locally within the workspace, typically to workspace-local users and groups. By default, users could access all data unless table access control was enabled at the cluster level, allowing admins or table owners to grant, revoke, or deny permissions for Hive metastore objects via SQL commands.
