-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC #### A2.2 Metastore Admins
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC The metastore admin role is a critical position in Unity Catalog, responsible for governing all securable objects and managing access controls at the metastore layer. This role is essential for robust data governance and operational flexibility across Databricks workspaces.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Metastore admins are responsible for the following tasks:
-- MAGIC - Creating catalogs, schemas, external locations, storage credentials, shares, and more.
-- MAGIC - Transferring ownership of any object, update metadata, set tags, and, if needed, grant themselves or others access—ensuring that no data asset is orphaned if team members leave or responsibilities change.
-- MAGIC - Adding managed storage to a metastore
-- MAGIC - Establishing connections to external data sources.
-- MAGIC - Delegating the ability to perform these tasks to others, via privilege grants.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Because of the importance of this role, being aware of and following these best practices is advised:
-- MAGIC - Assigning metastore admin rights to a group, preferably a platform operations or central governance team, is recommended over individual assignment for streamlined administration and continuity. 
-- MAGIC - Maintain role separation, since the metastore admin role complements account admin and workspace admin responsibilities. A clear delineation of roles supports clear workflows and escalation paths in larger organizations. 
-- MAGIC - Assign cautiously, since improper delegation risks overexposure of privileges, but lack of assignment can hamper critical operations in production.
-- MAGIC - Remember that some administrative tasks can be delegated to non-admin users via fine-grained privileges(such as `CREATE CATALOG`, `CREATE STORAGE CREDENTIAL`, and others); take this into consideration when designating admins

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## B. Managing Compute
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC To securely access Unity Catalog, clusters must be configured with the right access mode—specifically, standard or dedicated access modes on supported Databricks Runtime versions (11.3 LTS or above). Unity Catalog is secure by default, meaning that unless these access modes are selected, clusters cannot access Unity Catalog-managed data. This model enforces strict data permissions, centralizes governance, and prevents unintended data exposure independent of lower-level storage access 
-- MAGIC .
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC In contrast, legacy Hive metastore clusters require manual configuration. You must set Spark and Hive options, including versions and JARs, plus JDBC connection details through cluster settings or init scripts. Critically, in this "not safe by default" model, cluster-scoped credentials (like instance profiles, access keys, or service principal credentials) are broadly available to any user with cluster access, allowing them to bypass table-level security controls in Hive and directly access storage. This exposes the platform to risks because legacy clusters are open by default, and data controls can be circumvented unless explicitly tightened by admins. The “not safe by default” model also lacks the centralized, cross-workspace policy and lineage features provided by Unity Catalog

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### B1. Comparison of Hive Metastore to Unity Catalog
-- MAGIC

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC #### B1.1 Life Before UC
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Prior to Unity Catalog, all-purpose and job clusters required careful configuration to enforce data governance rules. Critically, in this "not safe by default" model, cluster-scoped credentials (like instance profiles, access keys, or service principal credentials) are broadly available to any user with cluster access, allowing them to potentially bypass table-level security controls in Hive and directly access storage. Without careful cluster configuration and policy enforcement, this exposes the platform to risks because legacy clusters were open by default, and data controls can be circumvented unless explicitly tightened by admins.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC #### B1.2 Life with UC
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC While there are still cluster configuration requirements to heed when implementing a system around UC, the critical distinction here is that, without these configurations, clusters will simply not be able to access Unity Catalog at all. Therefore, it is safe by default across all compute modes (including classic and serverless SQL warehouses and notebook and job compute).
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Specifically, the compute requirements (for classic compute) are:
-- MAGIC - **DBR:** 11.3 LTS or above
-- MAGIC - **Access mode:** *Standard* or *Dedicated*

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC ### B2. Enforcement of UC for Clusters
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC In a UC-enabled workspace running mixed (non-SQL) workloads, there are a few options to ensure users are able to access data and AI objects through UC. We walk through these options in this section.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC #### B2.1 Provision Clusters
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC In this approach, users are prevented from creating clusters. Rather, workspace admins pre-create them for the users:
-- MAGIC - Lock down cluster creation by disabling all policies and entitlements
-- MAGIC - Create a suitably configured cluster (or set of clusters) to share
-- MAGIC - Allocate `CAN RESTART` privileges granted to users to access the clusters
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC <div style="border-left: 4px solid #1976d2; background: #e3f2fd; padding: 16px 20px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 16px 0;">
-- MAGIC   <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px;">
-- MAGIC     <span style="font-size: 24px;">ℹ️</span>
-- MAGIC     <div>
-- MAGIC       This model only works with <i>Standard</i> access mode clusters, which restricts some functionality. Check the <a href="https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/standard-limitations" target="_blank">support matrix</a> to see if this is a viable approach for your organization.
-- MAGIC     </div>
-- MAGIC   </div>
-- MAGIC </div>
-- MAGIC

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC #### B2.2 Eliminate Classic Compute
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Since all serverless compute can access Unity catalog, elimination of classic compute provides a solution:
-- MAGIC - Lock down cluster creation by disabling all policies and entitlements
-- MAGIC - Instruct users to use serverless for their workloads
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC <div style="border-left: 4px solid #1976d2; background: #e3f2fd; padding: 16px 20px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 16px 0;">
-- MAGIC   <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px;">
-- MAGIC     <span style="font-size: 24px;">ℹ️</span>
-- MAGIC     <div>
-- MAGIC       Some functionality is only supported on classic compute; consult the <a href="https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/serverless/limitations" target="_blank">support matrix</a> to see if this is a viable approach for your organization.
-- MAGIC     </div>
-- MAGIC   </div>
-- MAGIC </div>

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC #### B2.3 Enforcement with a Compute Policy
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC The universal, recommended solution is to implement a policy allowing users to create their own compute, subject to restrictions set out in the policy. Workspace admins can set up a policy to restrict compute creation. Example settings are shown below:
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC <style>
-- MAGIC table{
-- MAGIC   border-collapse: separate;
-- MAGIC   border-spacing: 0;
-- MAGIC   width: auto;
-- MAGIC   max-width: 100%;
-- MAGIC   margin: 16px 0;
-- MAGIC   float: none;
-- MAGIC   clear: both;
-- MAGIC   display: table;
-- MAGIC   font-size: 0.95em;
-- MAGIC   line-height: 1.35;
-- MAGIC   background: #ffffff;
-- MAGIC   border-radius: 8px;
-- MAGIC   overflow: hidden;
-- MAGIC   box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px #303F47;
-- MAGIC   margin-left: 2px !important;
-- MAGIC   margin-right: 0px !important;
-- MAGIC   box-sizing: border-box !important;
-- MAGIC }
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC thead th{
-- MAGIC   background: #DADADA;
-- MAGIC   color: #1B3139;
-- MAGIC   font-weight: 600;
-- MAGIC   text-align: left;
-- MAGIC   padding: 10px 12px;
-- MAGIC }
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC tbody td{
-- MAGIC   padding: 10px 12px;
-- MAGIC   border-top: 1px solid #DCE0E2;
-- MAGIC }
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC tbody tr:nth-child(odd){
-- MAGIC   background: #FFFFFF !important;
-- MAGIC }
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC tbody tr:nth-child(even){
-- MAGIC   background: #F5F3F2 !important;
